irement and um i am  not convinced personally that the the  benefits of having a fully vaccinated  staff are going to outweigh the cost of  losing good people like this who are  really  um effective compassionate carers um  from both the care home system and the  hospital system we're already short  staffed and throughout the health and  care sector and i fear i hope i'm wrong  that this mandatory policy will only  make things worse they're big questions  of course whether it's survive all of  this survives legal tests i think as in  america there are lots of people going  to turn to the courts to overturn this  compulsion and so it's not yet clear to  me  that it will survive um  contact with the reality of the judicial  process yeah a debate that's set to  continue even though the health  secretary has made that decision for nhs  staff and care home workers and front  page jeff in paradise kezia this is  about the former attorney general sir  jeffrey cox who um went to the british  virgin islands to help with a corruption  case there and was voting remotely while  parliament was in session you could say  nice work if you can get it but i doubt  his constituencies in torridge west  devon will be feeling that way  no i mean what what a cracker of a  headline first and foremost from the  metro but you're right this is an  outrageous story at the extreme end of  the the scandal that's brewing now  around the number of mps with second  jobs this is a real extreme example of  an mp raking hundreds of thousands of  pounds at the other side of the world  as part of his second job and that's  been well rehearsed in the papers today  and i'm sure it will be for days to come  yet but the most angry thing about this  story from my perspective is the damage  it might do to the future of proxy  voting in parliament this is the system  whereby mps can cast their votes  electronically that was brought in  during the start of the pandemic we did  a survey of public opinion that john  smith centered about this and two thirds  of the public supported it they liked  the idea that it might help rule rural  or remote mps do their job a bit better  i think they hope that jeffy cox might  consider west devon to be the rural  remote location he'd vote for him rather  than the british virgin isles  um tim your view on sir jeffrey cox  voting from the british virgin islands  doesn't look good does it the  government's having quite the time of it  when it comes to  allegations of sleaze corruption second  jobs earning too much money and not  looking after  the public that have voted them into  those positions  it's a really messy situation for the  government and um i think um this is  going to obviously increase the whole  iron pressure public pressure for  whether mps should have second jobs  personally i think the house of  parliament  is enriched by having parliamentarians  who um aren't just sort of career  politicians uh but this uh story from  jeffrey cox obviously in the bvi um is  not a good advert for the case that  people like myself would like to be  making and tim we've only got 30 seconds  but we've got to talk about marcus  rashford mbes for mum's big example this  in the metro as well  well i'm a man united supporter you  can't really being accused of being  glory hunters at the moment there's not  much glory coming out of old trafford  but um marcus rashford is the exception  he's an exceptional footballer but also  just seems to be an exceptionally  big-hearted person as well and  i love the fact that he dedicated his  mbe as you were  clientele jane to his mum who was his  inspiration for the school dinners  campaign that has benefited so many uh  young children he is a great role model  isn't he tim thank you

